{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>You shall not kindle a fire in all your dwellings on the Sabbath day</b> The subject of this verse is certainly to prohibit on the Sabbath also the labor of food preparation, for it says <b>whoever does in it any melakhah shall be put to death</b>, and it specifies that they shall not kindle a fire even to bake bread and to cook meat, since fire is needed for every food. And it was necessary to say this because it did not say \"whoever does in it any melakhah\" as it said in the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:10) — \"you shall not do any melakhah\" — but rather said melakhah unspecified (סתם), and it would have been possible to exclude the labor of food preparation from the general rule, for thus it is stated regarding the Festival of Matzot (Deuteronomy 16:8) \"you shall not do melakhah\" and food preparation is not included in it. Therefore it explicitly mentioned that even food preparation is prohibited in it.<br>And I have found this same language in the Midrash (Mekhilta here): Rabbi Natan says, \"You shall not kindle a fire in all your dwellings on the Sabbath day\" — why is this stated? Because it says \"And Moses assembled all the congregation of the children of Israel,\" I might have thought it is permitted to light the lamp for him, to insulate hot food for him, and to make a bonfire for him on the Sabbath — therefore Scripture says \"You shall not kindle a fire in all your dwellings on the Sabbath day.\" And this is close to what we have said, that these labors that are for the benefit of one's body were not included in the original prohibition. And Rabbi Natan wanted to say that Scripture had no need to prohibit baking and cooking and other needs of food preparation, for He had already said to them \"that which you will bake, bake, and that which you will cook, cook\" (Exodus 16:23), but still all labors from which a person derives benefit and that produce only bodily enjoyment, such as lighting a lamp and a bonfire and washing one's body in hot water, would be permitted, for this is part of the delight of the Sabbath. Therefore \"you shall not kindle a fire\" is stated to prohibit everything.<br>And our Rages have a further midrash about it in the Talmud (Shabbat 70a): because it did not say \"whoever does in it any melakhah shall be put to death,\" or \"whoever kindles a fire in all your dwellings shall be put to death,\" they said that it came to lighten it, to make it a negative commandment (לאו). And some say that it came to separate, because it had been included in \"you shall not do any melakhah\" (Exodus 20:10)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The printed text reads וּלְרַבּוֹתֵינוּ, 'and our Rabbis,' translated literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:21:59.001540115Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}